# 1. Copy the template to create your local config
# cp .env.example .env.local

# 2. Open .env.local and swap out the placeholder with your active backend ngrok/local link!


# =====================================
# FilerNow — Environment Variables
# =====================================

# Backend integration (leads: Contact + Become-A-Filer
# backend is NOT running on this machine yet - testing against backend's
# ngrok tunnel for now. Ngrok free-tier URLs rotate every time he restarts
# the tunnel, so expect to update this line each session until he's on a
# stable host or you're running his backend locally.

# -------------------------------------
# Backend / FastAPI
# -------------------------------------

# 1.  Local development:

# FASTAPI_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:8000


# 2. Testing against backend developer's ngrok tunnel:

# No trailing slash — the route handler strips one defensively either way,
# but keep it clean here so copy-pasting a fresh ngrok link is a straight swap.
# FASTAPI_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:8000
FASTAPI_INTERNAL_URL=https://fritter-lance-amused.ngrok-free.dev


# 3. Production:

# Set this to the deployed/private FastAPI backend URL.

# FASTAPI_INTERNAL_URL=https://live-api-link


# -------------------------------------
# Authentication
# -------------------------------------

# Uncomment when the backend introduces a shared secret/JWT.
# Not in use yet — backend dev confirmed /api/admin/leads has no auth/token for
# now ("easy data flow for now"). Leave unset until he adds a shared secret
# or JWT requirement; uncomment and fill in when he does.

# JWT_PUBLIC_SECRET=replace-with-shared-secret-from-backend-dev


# -------------------------------------
# Public API
# -------------------------------------
# Optional.

# Leave unset/commented if the frontend is using mock data.
# Public API base (blog, filer status, etc. — if already in use)

# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=<his-ngrok-url-without-trail-last-slash>
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://pruritic-contemplable-roselee.ngrok-free.dev

# -------------------------------------
# Revalidation
# -------------------------------------
# Optional.

# Generate a strong random value if/when ISR/revalidation

# protection is implemented.

# REVALIDATE_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-secret


#--------------------------------------
# Production: set the same key in your hosting platform's env settings (Vercel → Project → Settings → Environment Variables), pointed at wherever FastAPI is actually deployed. If FastAPI sits behind a private network (same VPC, Railway internal networking, etc.), use the internal address — faster and never exposed to the public internet. If it's only reachable over a public URL, that's fine too, but then lock down /auth/* on the FastAPI side to only accept requests carrying a shared internal API key header, so randoms on the internet can't hit your auth endpoints directly by guessing the FastAPI URL.

# How it's used: only inside Route Handlers (app/api/auth/*/route.ts), never in a client component — fetch(${process.env.FASTAPI_INTERNAL_URL}/auth/login). Because it lacks the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, Next.js strips it from the client bundle at build time — it literally cannot leak to the browser.

# Handing this to your backend dev: don't share .env.local itself (it may end up with other secrets later). Instead, commit a .env.example with just the key names and dummy values, so they know what Next.js expects without you hand-typing it into a Slack message every time it changes: